---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="setup guide — tiny soft computer"
  description="Step-by-step setup instructions for building your own tiny soft computer."
  active="setup"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">setup guide</p>
    <h1>get your board talking to your display</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      what you'll need: an Adafruit RP2040 Feather ThinkInk, a 2.13" 212×104
      flexible monochrome eInk display (ILI0373 chipset), a USB-C cable,
      and a computer to program from.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>1. install CircuitPython</h2>
    <p class="note">
      before you start: disconnect the eInk display from the Feather.
      having it connected can interfere with the board entering
      bootloader mode. reconnect it once CircuitPython is installed.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>unplug the board completely.</li>
      <li>press and hold the <strong>BOOTSEL</strong> button.</li>
      <li>while still holding it, plug in the USB-C cable.</li>
      <li>wait a second or two, then release the button. this "boot on start" method is more reliable than double-pressing reset while already powered on.</li>
      <li>a drive named <code>RPI-RP2</code> should appear.</li>
      <li>go to <a href="https://circuitpython.org">circuitpython.org</a> and search for your exact board: "Adafruit Feather RP2040 ThinkInk". don't pick a generic RP2040 file, other variants have different pin mappings.</li>
      <li>choose the stable release (10.x), and download the single <code>.uf2</code> file.</li>
      <li>drag that file onto the <code>RPI-RP2</code> drive.</li>
      <li>the board restarts automatically. the drive is now named <code>CIRCUITPY</code>, that means CircuitPython is installed.</li>
      <li>now reconnect the eInk display.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      if the <code>RPI-RP2</code> drive never appears: confirm the display
      is disconnected, try a different USB-C cable (some are charge-only),
      try a different port, or unplug fully and retry the BOOTSEL sequence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>2. add the required libraries</h2>
    <p>
      from the <a href="https://circuitpython.org/libraries">CircuitPython
      Library Bundle</a>, matching your exact CircuitPython version, copy
      these into a <code>lib</code> folder on your CIRCUITPY drive:
    </p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><code>adafruit_il0373.mpy</code></li>
      <li><code>adafruit_display_text</code> (folder)</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      names must be exact, underscores not hyphens. a mismatched name is
      the most common reason a library fails to import.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>3. add the code</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>install <a href="https://codewith.mu/en/download">mu editor</a> to add the code </li>
      <li>copy <a href="/code">the code</a> onto your CIRCUITPY drive as <code>code.py</code>.</li>
      <li>the board should auto-run it. if not, press reset once.</li>
      <li>watch the serial console as it runs, it prints its progress at each step.</li>
      <li>wait for "refresh complete" before judging what's on screen. e-ink flickers through intermediate frames mid-refresh, this can look like smudging and is completely normal.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>troubleshooting notes</h2>
    <p>real issues we hit while testing, and what fixed them.</p>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>why the display can't refresh quickly</h3>
      <p>
        this isn't a bug, it's physics. e-ink works by physically moving
        charged ink particles inside each pixel, not by lighting up
        transistors. that movement takes real time, and it can't be
        rushed by better code.
      </p>
      <p>
        the manufacturer's own datasheet for this exact panel states it
        directly: <em>"each update interval time should be minimum at
        180 seconds"</em> for the panel's rated 5-year, 1,000,000-cycle
        lifespan to hold. the CircuitPython driver enforces this,
        attempting a refresh before that window passes raises a
        <code>RuntimeError: Refresh too soon</code>, on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        it's possible to override this in code (a <code>refresh_time</code>
        parameter exists), but doing so doesn't make the physical ink
        move any faster, it just lets the code ask again sooner than the
        panel can actually deliver. the likely result is a ghosted or
        incomplete image, and real wear on the panel over repeated
        cycles. we chose not to override it.
      </p>
      <p>
        this is exactly the kind of limitation this workshop asks you to
        design with, not around. rather than fighting the display's pace,
        the wait itself becomes part of what pressing the button means,
        not a flaw to hide.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>code runs with no error, but nothing shows</h3>
      <p>
        bitmaps must be created with <code>value_count=2</code>, not
        <code>1</code>. using <code>1</code> technically creates a
        zero-bit bitmap that can silently fail to render, even though
        the code runs without error. this was the root cause of a lot
        of confusing "it's not working" testing, not faulty hardware.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>screen looks blurry or smudged</h3>
      <p>
        e-ink flickers through several frames during a refresh. don't
        judge the screen until the code confirms the refresh is
        complete. checking mid-refresh looks exactly like a real
        problem, and usually isn't one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>the RPI-RP2 drive won't appear</h3>
      <p>
        disconnect the eInk display before entering bootloader mode.
        the display connected during this step can prevent the drive
        from showing up at all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>SyntaxError referencing &lt;stdin&gt;</h3>
      <p>
        not a code problem. usually a stray keypress sent into the REPL
        prompt directly. press Ctrl+D to reload, or press the physical
        reset button, and try again.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>a display seems consistently faulty</h3>
      <p>
        before assuming hardware failure: re-test with the latest known-good
        code first. an earlier bug in our own script caused some units to
        look broken when they weren't. if it's still inconsistent after
        that, isolate by swapping the display to a different board, and
        the board to a different display, whichever component the problem
        follows is the actual fault.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <p>
      this display holds its image with no power needed. once it shows
      text, you can even unplug it and the image will remain.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      this is a living document. it gets updated as we test more units
      and find new issues, so it stays accurate for whoever builds one
      after us.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>